    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">What Does a Small Boat Feel Like If You Have Never Been on One?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>28 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">It feels closer to the water than any ferry: a slow rise and fall when the boat stops, a firm and bouncy ride at speed, wind in your face, engine noise and the odd splash of spray. The front moves the most, so a seat at the back or in the middle is the steadiest place to start.</p>

      <h2>How is a small boat different from a ferry or a cruise ship?</h2><p>You feel the sea instead of watching it. A big ship is heavy enough to iron out most small waves. A 9 metre motor boat rides on top of them, so every change in the water reaches you through the seat, the floor and the rail under your hand.</p><p>That sounds alarming written down. In practice it is what makes a small boat fun. You sit close to the water, you smell the salt, and when a cliff like Cabo Girão rises over you, there is nothing between you and it. The trade is simple: more feeling, more view, less insulation.</p><h2>What does it feel like when the boat is running fast?</h2><p>Firm, bouncy and windy. At speed, the front of the boat lifts and the hull skims over the surface, tapping across the small waves rather than pushing through them. You feel each one as a knock, softer or harder depending on the day.</p><p>On a calm morning on the south coast, the ride can feel smooth and quick, almost like a car on a good road. On a day with some swell, it feels more like a country lane: steady rhythm, the occasional bigger bump, and a moment of lift when the boat meets a wave at an angle. Usually the quickest stretch is the last one, the ride home to Funchal, so that is when you feel the boat move most.</p><p>Your body helps more than you might think. Keep your knees soft, your back against the seat and one hand on a rail, and let the boat move under you instead of holding yourself rigid against it.</p><h2>What does it feel like when the boat stops?</h2><p>Quieter and slower, but not still. When the engine goes off at a swim stop such as Fajã dos Padres, the boat rocks gently from side to side as the swell passes under it. It is a lazy, rolling motion, very different from the knocks at speed.</p><p>This is the moment when people who are prone to seasickness notice it most, because the motion is slow and the eyes have nothing moving to follow. Looking at the horizon or at the cliffs, not at your phone, helps. So does getting in the water, if you swim. Our guide to <a href="/posts/seasickness-boat-trip-madeira-guide">seasickness on a boat trip in Madeira</a> covers the rest.</p><h2>Where should you sit on your first trip?</h2><p>At the back or in the middle, facing forward. In waves, the bow travels furthest up and down, and the back of the boat moves far less.</p><ul><li><strong>The back:</strong> the steadiest seat and a good view of the wake. The engine is usually loudest here.</li><li><strong>The middle, under the canopy:</strong> steady and shaded. The best seat if you are nervous or sensitive to sun.</li><li><strong>The front:</strong> the best view ahead and the most fun on a calm day, but the most bounce and the most spray when it is choppy. Try it at a stop first.</li></ul><p>Choose your seat before the boat leaves Marina do Funchal, and stay seated while it is running fast. Walking around is easy when it is stopped or moving slowly.</p><h2>Will you get wet?</h2><p>A little, perhaps. Spray comes off the front of the boat when it cuts through a wave, and the wind can carry a few drops back over the deck. On a calm day you may stay completely dry. On a breezy one, the front seats catch most of it.</p><p>A light layer you do not mind getting salty, and a bag for your phone, are enough. Leave anything precious at the hotel.</p><h2>How loud is it?</h2><p>Loud enough at speed that you raise your voice to talk, quiet enough at the stops that you hear water slapping against the rocks below the cliffs. Most of the trip alternates between the two. When the boat slows, for the drone shots at Cabo Girão or at a swim stop, the noise drops at once.</p><h2>How fast do you get used to it?</h2><p>Usually within the first part of the trip. The first stretch out of the marina is the strangest, because your body is still waiting for the ground to stop moving. By the time the boat reaches the first stop, the rhythm usually feels less foreign, and your hands loosen on the rail without you deciding to let go.</p><p>It also helps that the south coast is a good place for a first trip. The mountains in the middle of Madeira shelter this side of the island from the north-east trade winds, so the sea here is usually gentler than on the north coast. Our article on <a href="/posts/why-is-madeira-south-coast-calmer-for-boats">why the south coast is calmer for boats</a> explains the geography.</p><h2>When should your first trip be a short one?</h2><p>If the idea makes you anxious, you are prone to motion sickness or you are simply unsure, start short. The 2 hour Sunset Trip, which turns at Cabo Girão, is the shortest trip Chifbay runs, with no swimming and softer evening light.</p><p>If the forecast is rough, wait. Whether the sea is fit to go is the skipper's decision, and if the answer is no, the trip moves to another day or you are refunded in full. To read the sea yourself, try <a href="/posts/madeira-sea-conditions-boat-trip-guide">our notes on Madeira's sea conditions</a>. When you feel ready for the full coast, our <a href="/private-boat-tour-madeira">private boat tour in Madeira</a> page has both trips, and the Day Trip adds a swim at <a href="/faja-dos-padres-boat-trip">Fajã dos Padres</a>.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Can I stand up while the boat is moving?</h3><p>Stay seated while it runs fast. When it slows down or stops, walking around the deck is easy.</p><h3>Is a small boat safe on the open sea?</h3><p>Each day the skipper checks the sea before saying yes. Chifbay's licence as a maritime tourism operator is RNAAT n.º 305/2026.</p><h3>What shoes should I wear?</h3><p>Flat soles with some grip, or bare feet on deck. Heels and smooth leather soles slip on a wet deck.</p><h3>Does the boat lean when it turns?</h3><p>A little, into the turn, like a bicycle. It feels odd the first time and normal by the second.</p>

      <blockquote>Ten strange minutes, then the sea simply becomes part of the trip.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

